clark@knowideas.com (William Clark) writes:

> Hello there,
> 
> I am writing c code on a DEC Alpha box running DEC Unix.
> 
> I have created a daemon that forks a child to receive a file
> from a remote process.
> 
> Everything works great EXCEPT that I am collecting a zombie
> for each terminated child process.
> 
> I am using the signal function with SIGCHLD,SIG_IGN params before
> I start the loop that waits for a sockets connection and forks
> the child.
> 
> I thought that would prevent zombies.
> 
> Any ideas would be appreciated. Thanks in advance.
> 
> billc

Quite simply, the parent must issue a wait() system call for each
child that it forks.  A child will remain in a zombie state until
the parent collects its exit status with wait() (or wait3() or
wait4(); see "man 2 wait" for additional information).  Ignoring
SIGCHLD will not prevent zombies; in fact, it will be more likely
to preserve them.  Normally you would want to catch SIGCHLD and
issue a wait() from in the signal handler if you want to be able
to handle child exits asynchronously.

