William Clark wrote:

> Hello there,
>
> I am writing c code on a DEC Alpha box running DEC Unix.
>
> I have created a daemon that forks a child to receive a file
> from a remote process.
>
> Everything works great EXCEPT that I am collecting a zombie
> for each terminated child process.
>
> I am using the signal function with SIGCHLD,SIG_IGN params before
> I start the loop that waits for a sockets connection and forks
> the child.
>
> I thought that would prevent zombies.
>
> Any ideas would be appreciated. Thanks in advance.
>
> billc

 You're expecting System V semantics, and you don't get that by
default
for the signal() function on Digital UNIX. If you build in the UNIX 95

environment (using c89 instead of cc) or add -lsys5 to your link
command,
you'll get the behavior you're expecting.

However, the best way to do this is to switch to the POSIX sigaction
routine
using an sa_flags value of SA_NOCLDWAIT in your call for SIGCHLD.
This eliminates the need to call wait() as well. See the sigaction man
page.

Jeff
