Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Citicorp
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.lang.c


In DEC Unix (3.x and 4.x) I am finding that "free" does not release
memory back to the kernel so that it could be used by other processes.
This creates the problem that if several such processes are running each
needed a lot of memory at some time, none of them can share what the
others have used. Hence you run out of memory quickly.

I have a simple program below which illustrates the problem.
The program allocates 10Mb, then another 10Mb, then frees the second,
then frees the first. Both frees do not release the memory back to
kernel, though the same process could reuse its free memory.

I have tried tweeking some "__xxx" memory behaviour values but to no
avail. Has anyone got any ideas? 

Asgar Ali



Program:

#include <stdlib.h>
#include <iostream.h>



#define MEMSIZE 1024*1024*10            // 10M


/*
Defaults
__noshrink      = 0
 __minshrink    = 16384
 __minshrinkfactor = 0.001000
 __mingrow      = 49152
 __mingrowfactor = 0.100000
 __madvisor     = 0
 __small_buff   = 0
 __fast_free_max = 13
 __sbrk_override = 0
*/

int main( int argc, char* argv[] )
{
        char charbuf[128];

        char c;

        void* ptr;
        void* ptr2;


        printf("Allocating memory press enter ");
        scanf("%c", &c );
        ptr = malloc( MEMSIZE );

        printf("Allocating memory press enter ");
        scanf("%c", &c );
        ptr2 = malloc( MEMSIZE );

        printf("Press enter to free it ");
        scanf("%c", &c );
        free(ptr2);

        printf("Press enter to free it ");
        scanf("%c", &c );
        free(ptr);

        printf("Press enter to exit ");
        scanf("%c", &c );
        return 0;
}



