Ron Heiby wrote:

> I've re-tuned my kernel according to both suggestions I'd received. Sure
> enough, I am able to create more threads. However, there is a part of this
> experiment that still has me puzzled.
>

Digital Unix supports real-time scheduling of threads. There is also another feature of
Digital Unix which is called two-level scheduling. It basically maps user threads on to
kernel execution contexts (what are known as LWPs in Solaris). Kernel execution
contexts are the kernel threads. There can be any number of user threads mapping on
to kernel threads. The number of kernel threads is limited by Digital Unix configuration.
I feel you should take these factors into consideration, when you are playing with Digital Unix.

Regards
Srikanth





