--------------FC7C50AFC5EC590A230C358C
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

Dean J. wrote:

> I recently obtained nenscript to print code we're developing (ascii to
> postscript - to allow landscaped mode printing etc.). I've successfully
> compiled/installed the thing.
>
> My problem is that the printers (as they are currently configured) on
> the network don't seem to support Postscript characters. Instead, I just
> seem to be getting a postscript file printed out in its literal ascii
> representation (e.g. %%Title ..., %%Creator ... plenty of Postscript
> instructions, but nothing formatted).
>
> I originally configured the printers using the System
> Administrator/Printer Configuration tool which is part of Digital Unix
> 4.0D's system admin toolset.
>
> Is there someway I can rectify the problem, i.e. get my printers to
> support or interpret the Postscript files correctly?
>
> Many thanks, Dean J.



--------------FC7C50AFC5EC590A230C358C
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii; name="asdf.txt"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Content-Disposition: inline; filename="asdf.txt"


You didn't post the the printer type so I will assume it is an HP style
network printer with PostScript capabilities.

Print Queue Setup

	DON'T USE LPD REMOTE printing when you can use tcp/ip printing.

	HP JetDirect, and similar, cards provided tcp/ip socket
	capabilities. This feature was added in V4.0a and is a better way
	to go than lpd remote printing. Tcp/ip printing can use print 
	filters for file processing.

	More on this later.

About PostScript files

	Many programs expect PostScript files to start with %!. It is
	common for PostScript files created by PC's to add stuff before
	%! such as:

		<ctrl-d>%!
	or
		<esc>%-12345....%!

	When files don't start with %! the software attempts to convert
	the file to PostScript. I expect this is where you are having
	problems.

Converting text files to PostScript.

	The file /usr/lbin/ps_level1of is a print filter that will convert
	text to PostScript and pass PostScript files.  The program can 
	recognize PostScript files starting with %! or <ctrl-d>%! . The filter
	will assume files starting with <esc>%-12345....%! is a text file and
	attempt to convert it.

	This filter can print text files in landscape.  If I recall
	`lpr -w132' or `lpr -Olandscape' will provide landscape printing.
	If the printer has a duplex ability try lpr -K2.


Setting up a tcp/ip socket printer.

	This is documented in either the release notes or in the
	system admin guide.  Here are the key points.

	- You must be able to ping the printer.

	- You need to add the net card socket address in /etc/services.
	  For HP printers the address is usually 9100. So add something
	  like  `pr_9100 9100/tcp' to the end of the /etc/services file.
	  Restart inetd eg:

		> ps ax |grep inetd
		  537 ??       I        0:00.53 /usr/sbin/inetd
		21029 ttyp2    S  +     0:00.01 grep inetd
		> kill -HUP 537

	- Using lprsetup or printconfig create a print queue for a
	  ps_level1 printer.  In lprsetup for

		Set device pathname 'lp' [] ?"

	  specify

		@printer.ip.address/pr_9100

	- If everything went well you can print to the printer.

Sample Printcap Entry

	lp|lp0:\
		:if=/usr/lbin/ps_level1of:\
		:lf=/usr/adm/lp0err:\
		:mx#0:\
		:lp=@printer.dec.com/pr_9100:\
		:of=/usr/lbin/ps_level1of:\
		:pl#66:\
		:pw#80:\
		:rw:\
		:sd=/usr/spool/print/hp:\
		:xf=/usr/lbin/xf:


--------------FC7C50AFC5EC590A230C358C--




