In article <35616C36.9167165F@citicorp.com>
           asgar.ali@citicorp.com "Asgar Ali" writes:

>In DEC Unix (3.x and 4.x) I am finding that "free" does not release
>memory back to the kernel so that it could be used by other processes.
>This creates the problem that if several such processes are running each
>needed a lot of memory at some time, none of them can share what the
>others have used. Hence you run out of memory quickly.

On a VM system unused memory teds to get moves to swap space. As long as you
don't run out of swap space it may not be a big problem. 

>I have a simple program below which illustrates the problem.
>The program allocates 10Mb, then another 10Mb, then frees the second,
>then frees the first. Both frees do not release the memory back to
>kernel, though the same process could reuse its free memory.

That is typical. The C language doesn't require that freed memory be released
in this way, indeed it provides no answer to your question. You could try
finding a replacement malloc() library that does what you want or try to
find some platfor-specific alternative. For example many Unix platforms
these days support a mmap() system function which may well provide a solution.

