In article <35620a04.2342689789@207.126.101.81>, 
	kaz@cafe.dot.net (Kaz Kylheku) writes:

|> On Tue, 19 May 1998 12:25:42 +0100, 
|>         Asgar Ali <asgar.ali@citicorp.com> wrote:
|> 
|> >In DEC Unix (3.x and 4.x) I am finding that "free" does not release
|> >memory back to the kernel so that it could be used by other processes.
|> 
|> See Peter Seebach's Infrequently Asked Questions list:
|> 
|> 6.9: I have a program which mallocs but then frees a lot of memory, 
|> but memory usage (as reported by ps) doesn't seem to go back down. 
|> 
|>      You're probably not freeing the memory completely. Try replacing 
|> 
|>      free(foo);
|> 
|>      with 
|> 
|>      free(foo);
|>      free(foo);
|>      free(foo);
|> 
|> in case the first free() frees the memory only partially. (Unix wizards 
|> may recognize the parallel with syncing three times before rebooting.) 

This generally works (well, better than a single bare free(), that
is) but there are more robust ways to successfully free memory.
Some Unices have a difficult time freeing large chunks of memory,
and even the triple-free() approach above doesn't always work.

What I generally recommend is freeing the block one byte at a time:

    void progressive_free(void *ptr, size_t size)
    {
	size_t i;

	for(i = size - 1; i >= 1; --i)
	    ptr = realloc(ptr, i);
	free(ptr);
    }

This gradually chops the block down to a single byte, which most
implementations of free() handle quite nicely.  (Note the obvious
safety defect in that the return of realloc() is not checked and
the original pointer value is not preserved; sanity checking is
left as an exercise to the reader.)

Regards,

