Gerhard Kircher <gk@gkws.edvz.tuwien.ac.at> writes:

>Eric Werme - replace nobody with werme <nobody@zk3.dec.com> wrote:

:: I think the basic answer these days are that it doesn't matter, at
:: least not in any easy-to-optimize for fashion.  Disk these days
:: use zone recording where the number of sectors per track varies
:: between the inside and outside tracks to provide the same linear
:: density.  Outside tracks feature faster transfers and fewer seeks
:: than inside.

>O.K.  but  if  the  optimization  stuff is still being used,
>couldn't it make things worse by trying to  optimize  for  a
>wrong or fictious geometry?

Yes, at least for parts of the disk where the zone recording causes a
poor match.  If that is a problem, then in other areas of the disk it could
work better.

Combined with track readahead on the disks, large disk caches in the
OS, and servers with thousands of users, it's really hard to give you
a better answer.  Ultimately, the things that are important are to keep the
number of I/Os low and the number of seeks low.  Neither are very closely
coupled to historical sector layouts applied to current disks.
