Reply-To: (Replace nobody with werme) nobody@zk3.dec.com
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


mark@cita.toronto.edu (Mark Bartelt) writes:

>One thing I've wondered about for a long time, though (and
>my attempts to RTFM haven't provided an answer) is whether
>it's important (and, if so, how important) that things like
>the rpm value and the {track,cylinder}skew values be right.

I think the basic answer these days are that it doesn't matter, at
least not in any easy-to-optimize for fashion.  Disk these days
use zone recording where the number of sectors per track varies
between the inside and outside tracks to provide the same linear
density.  Outside tracks feature faster transfers and fewer seeks
than inside.

The actual architecture is well hidden by SCSI.  All the OS is
asking for these days is block X, instead issuing seeks and reading
from particular surfaces.  There is some vestigial stuff in newfs
for dealing with the old parameters, but they only really come into
play between cylinders, and cylinders are a lot bigger than they used
to be, so those optimizations are far less important that they
used to be.

Yeah, there's stuff that could be cleaned up there, but there are
bigger things we could be working on too.  While I don't work
directly on UFS, I do worry about NFS performance on servers using
UFS, and I've decided not to worry about the skew data, except on
days I can't find anything else to worry about.
