I've re-tuned my kernel according to both suggestions I'd received. Sure
enough, I am able to create more threads. However, there is a part of this
experiment that still has me puzzled.

When I started, I knew that the system tunable parameter limit on the number
of threads I could have would stop me before I was anywhere near where I
wanted to test. So, I configured max-proc-per-user to 4000. The command
"/sbin/sysconfig -q proc" confirmed that it (and max-threads-per-user) was
set to 4000.

After receiving the advice here on the other tunables to increase, I decided
that I wanted to push things a bit, and try up to 6000 threads. So, I
re-configured max-proc-per-user to 8000. When I again did a "/sbin/sysconfig
-q proc", I expected to see 8000 for max-proc-per-user and
max-threads-per-user, but I did not. Instead, each was set to 4107.

Now, I think that 4107 is an interesting number for two reasons.

1) It isn't what I asked for.
2) It isn't a power of 2 (loved by us computer types).

Instead, it's (37 * 37 * 3). I don't get it.

1) Why isn't my system getting tuned for the values I'm setting?
2) How do I tune my system for more than 4107 threads?

Thanks!




