In article <354E72D5.277D0975@pt.lu> Edouard Lauer at P & T
<elauer@pt.lu> writes:
>i.e /usr/spool/mail/e4901 is a symbolic link to /PROD/users/mail/e4901
>
>Problem is that sendmail apparently doesn't handle this

Correct, sendmail doesn't handle this - it has nothing to do with user
mailboxes. 

>    binmail: /usr/spool/mail/e4901 has more than one link or is a
>symbolic link

As you can see, the complaint comes from a program that calls itself
"binmail" - presumably that's /bin/mail on your system; in any case it's
the mail delivery agent specified in the Mlocal definition in your
sendmail.cf.

It probably has good cause to complain, as it is probably setuid-root,
and following a symlink, that might have been created by a malicious
user, when writing the mail would probably be a very bad idea.

You may have better luck making a symlink from /usr/spool/mail to
/PROD/users/mail, or perhaps better still mounting /usr/spool/mail
wherever you want it.

