> According to the manual, that is, indeed, the parameter involved in this.
>
> My confusion is that my parameter is currently set at the default value of
> 200. Yet, I'm managing to create over 3700 threads before getting into
> trouble. I would think that each thread would require 2 of the 200
> vm-mapentries (at least) -- one for the stack and one for the guard. This
> would tend to say to me that I should have been able to create just under
> 100 threads. I hope that the source of my confusion is evident.

The vpagemax value is what's helping you here. The issue
is the creation of guard pages (red zones) for each stack
to detect stack overrun. The guard pages (which come
out of the heap like the rest of the thread stack) are
marked no-access, of course. I believe in the original Mach
VM (2.5 era), that would have forced the creation of
a new map entry, because of the "discontiguous" nature
of changing the guard page's protection in the midst
of all those read/write pages. You'd chew up a lot of map
entries this way with lots of threads! A big lists of map
entries can cause long list traversals in certain situations.

The Digital rewrite of the Mach VM for DEC OSF/1 V1.2,
introduced the vpagemax concept to avoid increasing
the number of map entries in response to "minor"
changes in a group of otherwise like pages of VM.
Instead of creating a new map entry, a virtual page
list is created in the entry to hold up to 'vpagemax'
page descriptors for the pages in the VM range covered
by the map entry. This list is much easier to manage
than map entries, and allows pages with, say, different protections to coexist
side-by-side in the map entry.

Anyway, in spite of this murky description, I want to
make clear that the vm-vpagemax parameter is much more
likely to be involved in thread-creation problems
than vm-mapentries is.

[That said, I should muddy the waters even more by saying that
in an upcoming release of Digital UNIX, that story is reversed with the advent
of a completely new method of creating user
thread stacks. These nifty little stack objects will make
life much easier by allowing huge thread stacks at much
lower cost in VM -- they won't take up VM/swap resources
for pages you're not using. If I recall, the default
stack size will be 5 meg. Something like that...]

Apologies in advance for any distorted facts and recollections!

